Securing the Web and File Transfers: HTTPS and FTPS

Every network protocol is built around two basic ideas. First, each protocol belongs to specific layers of the OSI or TCP/IP model and behaves according to the functions of those layers. 
Second, protocols based on TCP or UDP operate on specific network ports. With these ideas in mind, two widely used secure protocols can be examined: HTTPS for web traffic and FTPS for file transfers. 
Both share the same core principle: they take an older, unencrypted protocol and add a layer of encryption to protect the data.

HTTPS, or Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure, is a client-server protocol that securely carries data between a web browser and a website. 
It is the encrypted version of HTTP, which sends everything in cleartext over TCP port 80. In HTTP, a user agent, such as a browser or an application using an API, sends requests to a web server. 
A GET request asks for a resource like a page, an image or a file, while POST and PUT send data to the server. The problem is that anyone who captures the packets between client and server can read their contents. 
For simply reading about a company's products this may be acceptable, but for entering a password or credit card number it is not. HTTPS solves this by adding SSL/TLS on top of HTTP and using port 443 by default. 
TLS combines two types of encryption: asymmetric encryption, which uses a public and a private key, is used to negotiate a symmetric algorithm and a shared secret key, and symmetric encryption then protects the actual traffic. 
As a result, an attacker who intercepts the packets cannot read or alter them, which preserves both confidentiality and integrity. Without HTTPS, online banking and online payments would not be possible.

FTP, the File Transfer Protocol, is much older, dating back to 1971 and RFC 114, and was later refined in RFC 765 and RFC 959.
It also follows the client-server model and authenticates users with a username and password, but both the login and the files travel unencrypted. 
FTP uses two separate channels: a control connection on TCP port 21 for commands and authentication, and a data connection for transferring files and folders. 
It can work in two modes. In active mode, the client opens the control connection, and then the server connects back to the client from port 20 to send data. 
This fails when the client is behind a firewall that blocks incoming connections. 
In passive mode, the client sends the PASV command, the server replies with a random port, and the client opens the data connection itself, which works well behind firewalls. 
FTP also supports several data types: ASCII (Type A) for text, Image (Type I) for binary byte-by-byte transfer, EBCDIC (Type E) for that character set, and Local Type L n for systems without 8-bit bytes.

FTPS extends FTP by adding TLS to both the command and data channels. It can be used in two ways. In implicit FTPS, the connection is encrypted automatically from the start and uses port 990. 
In explicit FTPS, the client connects on port 21 and explicitly asks the server to start a TLS session; the client can then choose to encrypt only the control channel, only the data channel, or both. 
In either case, FTPS protects login credentials and transferred files from sniffing attacks.

In conclusion, HTTPS and FTPS show how security is often added to existing protocols rather than built from scratch. 
By wrapping HTTP and FTP in TLS and moving them to their own ports, they keep the original workflow intact while protecting the confidentiality and integrity of the data, which is essential for modern online services.

Secure Application-Layer Protocols: DNSSEC, OpenPGP, SSH, SMTPS and POP3S

The Domain Name System (DNS) translates human-friendly domain names into IP addresses. For example, example.com resolved to 93.184.216.34, which is much harder to remember than the name. 
A client sends DNS queries for specific record types: an A record for an IPv4 address and an AAAA record for an IPv6 address. 
A tshark capture on port 53 shows host 192.168.0.102 sending A and AAAA queries for example.com to the DNS server 1.0.0.1 and receiving the IPv4 address 93.184.216.34 and the IPv6 address 2606:2800:220:1:248:1893:25c8:1946. 
The weakness is that anyone on the network could send a forged answer, and the host would accept any response and could connect to a rogue server. 
DNSSEC addresses this. The zone owner signs all DNS records with a private key and publishes a public key so clients can verify the signatures. This gives two guarantees. 
Authenticity means the record really comes from the domain owner. Integrity means the record was not changed in transit, because any change makes the signature invalid.

Email was designed in a different era. 
The first email was sent in 1971, and SMTP (sending), POP3 (downloading) and IMAP (mailbox synchronization) all work in cleartext, like a postcard anyone can read as it passes between servers. 
Webmail first ran over HTTP, which had the same confidentiality and integrity problems. It later moved to HTTPS, which encrypted the link between the browser and the web server. 
However, traffic between the web server and the mail servers was not necessarily encrypted, and servers could still read messages. 
SSL/TLS was then added to the mail protocols, producing SMTPS, POP3S and IMAPS, where the “S” means secure. Even so, mail servers along the way can still read message contents, so users must trust them.

For end-to-end protection, the answer is OpenPGP. PGP (Pretty Good Privacy) was created by Phil Zimmermann. OpenPGP is the open standard for signing and encrypting files and email, defined in RFC 4880. 
GnuPG (GPG) is a free, open-source implementation of it that integrates with mail clients. Each user generates a key pair with gpg --gen-key, which asks for a name and email address. 
The private key is kept secret, and the public key is shared. The sender signs with their own private key and encrypts with the recipient’s public key. 
The recipient verifies the signature with the sender’s public key and decrypts with their own private key. As a result, nobody except the recipient, not even the mail servers, can read the content. 
Email headers are not protected, though. On the command line, a message is encrypted with gpg --encrypt --sign --armor -r strategos@tryhackme.thm message.txt:

--encrypt -r encrypts with the recipient’s public key, which provides confidentiality.
--sign signs with the sender’s private key, which provides authenticity.
--armor produces ASCII output instead of binary.

The result is a -----BEGIN PGP MESSAGE----- block that can be sent in any email.

Remote administration followed a similar path. Telnet and rlogin let users log in over a network, but they protected neither confidentiality nor integrity. 
An attacker monitoring the network could read credentials and even modify commands. Running Wireshark’s “Follow TCP Stream” on a Telnet session reveals the username michael and the password RJ9wn^t3T%gC in plain text. 
The password is strong, but it is useless when sent like that. SSH fixed this. 
In a captured SSH session, only the version numbers and the supported algorithms are visible, and no usernames, passwords or commands can be seen.

SMTPS is SMTP wrapped in TLS. It provides authentication, integrity and confidentiality. SMTP is an “email push protocol” that moves mail from a client to a server. 
POP3, in contrast, downloads mail from the server to the client. SMTP works in two models. End-to-end is used between organizations: the sender’s SMTP client connects directly to the recipient’s SMTP server. 
Store-and-forward is used within an organization: the server keeps a copy of the message until it forwards it. 
The key components are the User Agent (UA), which creates the message and passes it to the Mail Transfer Agent (MTA), which carries it across the Internet to the recipient’s MTA. 
The MTA and the Mail Delivery Agent (MDA) are often on the same server. Encryption is switched on with the STARTTLS command. SMTPS commonly uses ports 587 and 465. 
It protects against sniffing, and the source also claims it hinders spam from compromised domains, data exfiltration and phishing.

POP3S is the secure version of POP3. POP3 retrieves mail from the MDA to the MUA (Mail User Agent) as follows:

The client connects to the server.
It downloads all queued messages (or only selected ones).
The messages are stored on the device that connected.
By default, the server deletes its copy.

POP3 has two main limitations. There is no synchronization across devices, and credentials and messages travel in cleartext, which makes them vulnerable to sniffing. 
POP3S wraps the session in TLS, switched on via STARTTLS. POP3S uses port 995, while POP3 uses port 110.
